#   Keeps calibration submission asynchronous and returns the HID transport error or the sysfs byte count.
#   Adds a common error reader that validates controller and module selectors, reads once, and emits a fixed-width hexadecimal 16-bit value.
# Why: Start and Stop are explicit one-byte firmware actions. A command-to-module map is required before the driver can reset the correct result.
#   A new Start must not expose the previous run as its result. Stop is cancellation and must not pretend that a new run started.
#   Completion arrives later through input, so status and error have unsynchronized readers and writers. Userspace needs the firmware error separately from success or failure status.
